live-for-speed-linux for Debian
================================

This package contains only the free community launcher, integrity manifests,
desktop metadata, documentation, and original artwork. It does not contain
Live for Speed, Wine, DXVK, Windows executables, account data, or player data.
It is in contrib because normal use requires proprietary Live for Speed files
that are not distributed by Debian.

Live for Speed 0.8C20 new graphics is a PUBLIC TEST, not a stable game release.
The launcher is unofficial and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Live
for Speed developers.

Downloads and terms
-------------------

Package installation, upgrade, removal, desktop-menu refresh, and AppStream
processing perform no upstream downloads. After an explicit unprivileged
`lfs-linux install` command or informed desktop confirmation, the launcher
downloads and verifies:

* the official Live for Speed installer from https://www.lfs.net/;
* Wine 11.15-1 and its detached signature from the immutable Arch Linux
  Archive; and
* DXVK 3.0.2 from its official GitHub release.

Exact URLs, byte sizes, SHA-256 digests, the exact Arch packager certificate
and fingerprint, and protected payload manifests ship in /usr/share/lfs-linux.
The Wine package signature is checked with `gpgv`. Archive member paths and
links are preflighted before extraction. A changed, unsafe, or incomplete
payload fails closed before extraction or execution. Live for Speed remains governed by
https://www.lfs.net/agreement. Wine is LGPL-2.1-or-later; DXVK uses the Zlib
license. No downloaded payload is redistributed by this Debian package.

The exact Arch Wine package corresponds to packaging tag 11.15-1 and commit
f70b597ed89e4e0beae53ace69b109eaff274648:
https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/packaging/packages/wine/-/tree/11.15-1
The Arch recipe builds signed upstream Wine tag wine-11.15 from:
https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/tree/wine-11.15

User state and removal
----------------------

Runtime files are stored under the invoking user's XDG data, cache, and state
directories. The launcher must not be run as root. Normal APT removal deletes
only Debian-managed files under /usr and preserves profiles, settings,
replays, mods, game files, caches, and local update baselines. Users who also
want to remove those files must run `lfs-linux remove` and
`lfs-linux purge-cache` explicitly before uninstalling the package.
